Subject: Represent std::array<T, N> as the Rust array [T; N]

bindgen cannot model a class template with a non-type parameter, and
`std::array`'s definition is one: its member has type `T[N]`. So the template
declaration is opaque, `TemplateInstantiation::is_opaque` reports every
specialization of it opaque in turn, and codegen writes each as a blob of bytes
of the right size and alignment.

What that costs is the element type. `__BindgenOpaqueArray<[u32; 3usize]>` is
what a caller is told about `std::array<uint32_t, 3>`, about
`std::array<Elem, 3>` for a four-byte `Elem`, and about any other twelve-byte
class laid out to four-byte alignment - three different C++ types with one
Rust spelling and nothing in it to tell them apart. A signature mentioning one
cannot be bound: the blob is a different type from the one C++ wrote.

The standard makes `std::array<T, N>` an aggregate holding those `N` `T`s
contiguously, so Rust's `[T; N]` is that object, with that layout. Under
`--represent-std-array`, emit it.

The two facts this needs are ones libclang will give and bindgen was not
asking for. `clang_Type_getTemplateArgumentAsType` reports a non-type argument
as an invalid type and `TemplateInstantiation::from_ty` drops it, so `N` is not
reachable from the type; `clang_Cursor_getNumTemplateArguments` and its
companions report every argument of the specialization's cursor with its kind,
and an integral one with its value. `Cursor::template_arguments` is that, and
`std_array_shape` reads the pair `std::array` is written with.

The substitution is made only where `[T; N]` names the same C++ type back.
`sizeof(std::array<T, N>)` is not mandated to be `N * sizeof(T)`, so the size
and alignment clang reports for the specialization are compared against the
array's, and an implementation which laid one out otherwise keeps today's blob.
`N == 0` never matches: a zero-length `std::array` is an object with a size and
`[T; 0]` has none. Nor does an element which is itself a C array, since
`std::array<uint8_t[2], 3>` and `std::array<std::array<uint8_t, 2>, 3>` are
different classes with the same layout, or a `const` or `volatile` one, which
is a template argument Rust cannot spell.

The decay rule for an array parameter learns the difference at the same time. C
adjusts a parameter declared as an array to a pointer, and a class passed by
value is not adjusted; a C++ array type has no declaration to take a name from,
so the name this puts on the array is what tells the two apart.

Off by default: for anyone reading `std::array` out of a struct field today,
the blob and the array occupy the same bytes but are not the same Rust type.

